The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': {'THELOOK_ECOMMERCE': ['DISTRIBUTION_CENTERS', 'ORDERS', 'USERS', 'EVENTS', 'INVENTORY_ITEMS', 'PRODUCTS', 'ORDER_ITEMS']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables, columns, and why they matter  

• THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.ORDER_ITEMS  
  – "product_id": to aggregate the number of items purchased.  
  – "id": row identifier (each row ≈ one item purchased).  
  – "status": (optional) filter to keep rows that represent real sales, e.g. ‘Complete’ or ‘Shipped’.  
  – "user_id": needed later to tie an order back to the customer if desired.  
  – "created_at": order‑item creation time (μs‐epoch).  

• THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.PRODUCTS  
  – "id": primary key, joins to ORDER_ITEMS."product_id".  
  – "category": product category we need to rank by quantity.  

• THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.EVENTS  
  – "session_id": to order events within a single browsing session.  
  – "created_at": μs‑epoch timestamp of each event; will be used to compute time deltas.  
  – "uri": lets us isolate page views that look like product pages (e.g. uri ILIKE '%/product/%').  
  – "event_type": may also indicate a product‑view event; first look at its distinct values.  
  – "user_id": (optional) can join back to USERS or reconciled with ORDER_ITEMS.  

Typical conditions you may want  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped') to ignore cancelled items.  
• EVENTS."uri" ILIKE '%/product/%' or EVENTS."event_type" = 'view_product' to pick product page events.  
• When calculating dwell time: use LEAD("created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY "session_id" ORDER BY "created_at").  
• Time‑difference in minutes: (next_event – product_page_event) / 60000000.0  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake queries (LIMIT 20 each) that progressively build understanding, all with double‑quoted identifiers.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: List a sample of distinct product categories available.
SELECT DISTINCT "category"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Check how many order‑item rows exist per category (unfiltered).
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(oi."id") AS "item_qty"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"  oi
JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"     p
  ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "item_qty" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Repeat #2 but keep only successful sales (Complete or Shipped).
SELECT p."category",
       COUNT(oi."id") AS "item_qty"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"  oi
JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"     p
  ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
WHERE oi."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped')
GROUP BY p."category"
ORDER BY "item_qty" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Preview distinct event_type values to discover product‑view indicators.
SELECT DISTINCT "event_type"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: See a sample of URIs that look like product pages.
SELECT "uri", "event_type", "created_at"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Pull all events within one random session that contains product views.
SELECT *
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
WHERE "session_id" = (
      SELECT "session_id"
      FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
      WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
      LIMIT 1
)
ORDER BY "created_at"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: For all product‑page events, calculate the time until the next event in minutes.
SELECT "session_id",
       "uri",
       "created_at",
       ( LEAD("created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY "session_id" ORDER BY "created_at")
         - "created_at") / 60000000.0 AS "minutes_on_page"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Average minutes‑on‑page for every category by joining EVENT uris to PRODUCTS.sku pattern (*heuristic*).
--   Assumes product SKU appears in the URI; first quickly inspect if that is true.
SELECT e."uri"
FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS" e
WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Compute average minutes‑on‑page for *all* product page views (no category split yet).
WITH page AS (
  SELECT "session_id",
         ( LEAD("created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY "session_id" ORDER BY "created_at")
           - "created_at") / 60000000.0 AS "minutes_on_page"
  FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS"
  WHERE "uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
)
SELECT AVG("minutes_on_page") AS "avg_minutes_on_page"
FROM page
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Illustration of the final combined logic—
--     get the highest‑quantity category (from #3) and then
--     calculate the average minutes‑on‑page just for that category.
--     (Replace 'Accessories' with the real top category once identified.)
WITH top_cat AS (
    SELECT p."category"
    FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."ORDER_ITEMS"  oi
    JOIN "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."PRODUCTS"     p
      ON oi."product_id" = p."id"
    WHERE oi."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped')
    GROUP BY p."category"
    ORDER BY COUNT(oi."id") DESC
    LIMIT 1
),
page_views AS (
    /* Product page events whose URI contains the category keyword
       (adjust the URI filter if pattern is different). */
    SELECT e."session_id",
           ( LEAD(e."created_at") OVER (PARTITION BY e."session_id" ORDER BY e."created_at")
             - e."created_at") / 60000000.0 AS "minutes_on_page"
    FROM "THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."THELOOK_ECOMMERCE"."EVENTS" e,
         top_cat tc
    WHERE e."uri" ILIKE '%' || tc."category" || '%'
      AND e."uri" ILIKE '%/product/%'
)
SELECT tc."category" AS "top_category",
       AVG(pv."minutes_on_page") AS "avg_minutes_on_page"
FROM top_cat tc
LEFT JOIN page_views pv ON 1=1
GROUP BY tc."category"
LIMIT 20;
```

